tive of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096411 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096411 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096411 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \n                          f'(x)             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right)\\\n                                             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\n\t\t\t\t\t     &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\t\t\t\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  12x^{3} - 2x.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096412 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096412 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096412 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480008 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480009 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480011 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480014 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480016 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480017 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480023 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480027 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480034 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480041 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480044 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480048 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096610 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921860