edb/ref 1059 90.0 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce63fcf4-9592-481e-982a-405b6f12d7be" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186479960 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480003 #edb/ref 1059 90.0 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fab70c36-4b77-4dae-b226-e0c0429677ee" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186479993 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480008 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480012 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480008 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480008 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡3$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480008 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24285fdc-6262-5d37-b204-181d4efdc6b1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480008 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480033 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480009 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 + 2e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27963039-7ab9-50a1-a524-335dda2120d3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480009 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480042 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480011 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3368f5c9-3775-5d06-9195-e0e05c5824a2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480011 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480046 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480014 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40eee14f-7f06-5ca0-8856-ee0dc15c1493" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480014 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡9$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(7{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =7{e}^{1} \\\\  & =7 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480019 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480016 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡9$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5104b80b-063d-53dc-91b3-3f3e99aa0687" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480016 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡9} & ={(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480021 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480017 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5184f338-d6d0-533d-88bb-8b37da109e6c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480017 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡2} & ={(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480037 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480023 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f698a42-ae62-5802-8f77-88360f877560" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480023 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} & ={(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480047 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480027 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78a2b5f7-5cef-5012-a53c-264a4e19606b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186480027 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
